\mathcal{O}(G),&amp;L_{p}\gg d\end{cases}\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1.1pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.5.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{aligned} \approx\begin{cases}\mathcal{O}(BL),&amp;L_{p}\ll d\\
\mathcal{O}(L),&amp;L_{p}\gg d\end{cases}\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.700em">≈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">L</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">L</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">≪</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">L</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">L</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">≫</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{aligned} \approx\begin{cases}\mathcal{O}(BL),&amp;L_{p}\ll d\\
\mathcal{O}(L),&amp;L_{p}\gg d\end{cases}\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Asymptotic Analysis of Arithmetic Intensity in ARM and DLM ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows asymptotic analysis of arithmetic intensity (AI) for ARMs and DLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ARMs.</span>
Prefill shifts from <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">linear-projection</em>-dominated AI (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(BL_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(BL_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>) when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}\ll d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}\ll d</annotation></semantics></math> to <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attention</em>-dominated AI (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(L_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(L_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>) when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}\gg d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}\gg d</annotation></semantics></math>.
During the subsequent decode phase, each step involves small matrix multiplications (<em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">linear projections</em>) and retrieval of the growing KV cache for <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attention</em>. As a result, the AI is initially <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}\ll d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}\ll d</annotation></semantics></math>, but it drops to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> once <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}\gg d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}\gg d</annotation></semantics></math>, since the memory load from attention begins to dominate the fixed per-token compute; see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Block-DLMs.</span>
In a block-wise DLM with block size <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, each refinement step updates a block of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in parallel. The parallel computation within its linear layers is analogous to an ARM prefill operation on a sequence of length <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the attention mechanism is fundamentally distinct, as these G tokens attend to the KV cache representing the entire sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. The one-time cost of initializing this cache via a full-attention pass is omitted, as it is a less dominant term compared to the cumulative cost of the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> refinement steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Naive DLMs.</span>
In contrast, a naive (fully bidirectional) DLM processes the full sequence each step; the scale factor is the total length <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=L_{p}+L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=L_{p}+L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, giving <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{AI}\approx\mathcal{O}(BL)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>AI</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{AI}\approx\mathcal{O}(BL)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}\ll d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}\ll d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}\gg d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}\gg d</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, when KV caching is absent, each sampling step recomputes the full sequence, akin to ARM prefill, and is therefore typically compute-bound.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Runtime and Roofline Analysis of ARM and DLM</h3>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04146v1/runtime_updated.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:462/204;" width="462" height="204"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtime comparison of ARM and DLM.</span>
(a) End-to-end runtime vs. generation length <math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for a short context (<math id="S3.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}=128</annotation></semantics></math>); bars show ARM prefill/decode and the line shows naive DLM total.
(b) Same as (a) but for a long context (<math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}=2\mathrm{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}=2\mathrm{k}</annotation></semantics></math>).
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04146v1/roofline_updated.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/154;" width="238" height="154"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Roofline comparison of ARM and DLM.</span>
Roofline placement across sequence lengths for ARM prefill, ARM decode, and naive DLM, simulated for an NVIDIA RTX A6000 with a peak performance of 154.8 TFLOP/s and a ridge point at an AI of 201.6 FLOP/Byte.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study end-to-end runtime for ARMs and DLMs as generation length <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> varies under two prompt lengths, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}{=}128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}{=}128</annotation></semantics></math> (short) and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}{=}2\mathrm{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}{=}2\mathrm{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (long).
We use batch size <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>, do <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> use block-wise decoding and approximate caching, and set the number of diffusion steps to match the generation length, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, a common convention in diffusion language models.
The roofline analysis assumes a peak FP16 performance of 154.8 TFLOP/s for the A6000 GPU (see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A RTX A6000 Roofline Parameters ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for details) and models LLaMA-3 with a 2k-token sequence length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.3 Runtime and Roofline Analysis of ARM and DLM ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (a) shows latency for the short prompt.
The ARM runtime decomposes into prefill (blue), which is flat in <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, and decode (red), which grows modestly because KV caching prevents recomputation.
The DLM is quite fast for short prompts and small <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, thanks to parallel token updates, but its latency rises sharply as <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> grows.
With a long prompt in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.3 Runtime and Roofline Analysis of ARM and DLM ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (b), DLM latency is higher even at small <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, since each sampling step recomputes the entire sequence, whereas ARM decode reuses the cached tokens from prior steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.3 Runtime and Roofline Analysis of ARM and DLM ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> places these workloads on the roofline. ARM prefill (red) lies to the right of the ridge point and is <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compute-bound</span>, reflecting large GEMMs over the full prompt. ARM decode (blue) sits on the bandwidth roof and is <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">memory-bound</span>, dominated by KV-cache traffic. For naive DLM (green), short sequences (e.g., up to 128 tokens) lie near the bandwidth roof, while longer sequences cross the ridge point and become <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compute-bound</span> as full-sequence denoising dominates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Implications of Block-wise Decoding with KV Cache</h3>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04146v1/blockwise_updated.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/181;" width="476" height="181"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
<span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtime and AI for naive and block-wise DLM with KV caching.</span>
(a) End-to-end runtime vs. generation length <math id="S3.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> at a fixed prompt <math id="S3.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}=1\mathrm{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}=1\mathrm{k}</annotation></semantics></math>; bars show ARM (for reference); lines show the naive DLM ("No Blocks") and block-wise DLM for block sizes <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\{32,64,128\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\{32,64,128\}</annotation></semantics></math> with diffusion steps <math id="S3.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(b) Aggregate AI vs. <math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for the naive and block-wise DLM configurations.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Block-wise decoding</span> combines the strengths of ARMs and DLMs: it reuses the KV cache across completed blocks (ARM-like), while updating all tokens within the active block in parallel (DLM-like).
The diffusion steps <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are typically distributed evenly across blocks.
We conduct a profiled analysis, where we fix the prompt length to <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}=1\mathrm{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}=1\mathrm{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider block sizes <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\{32,64,128\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\{32,64,128\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and set the number of diffusion steps equal to the generation length (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.4 Implications of Block-wise Decoding with KV Cache ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (a) compares end-to-end latency versus generation length <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating how block-wise decoding with KV caching reduces latency by roughly <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times</annotation></semantics></math>, compared to naive DLM (labeled "No Blocks").
Moreover, Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.4 Implications of Block-wise Decoding with KV Cache ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (b) shows that this approach not only reduces the AI but also makes it invariant to the generation length, depending only on the block size <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
This reduction in AI translates into lower latency in compute-bound settings (e.g., naive DLM inference in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.4 Implications of Block-wise Decoding with KV Cache ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (b)), and block-wise decoding reduces the required FLOPs relative to naive DLM (as highlited in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Asymptotic Analysis of Arithmetic Intensity in ARM and DLM ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that even with KV caching, the DLM remains slower than the ARM at matched prompt and output lengths when <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> scales with <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, the widely used configuration of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> implies that the model denoises and finalizes only one token per step on average, which is hugely inefficient. This underscores the importance of <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reducing diffusion steps</span> via efficient multi-token denoising, a point we revisit in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Discussion and Future Work ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Implications of Inference with Different Batch Sizes</h3>

<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04146v1/batching_updated.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/146;" width="476" height="146"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
<span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Throughput vs. batch size for ARM and block-wise DLM.</span>
Throughput (tokens/s) as a function of batch size <math id="S3.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> for (a) short prompt (<math id="S3.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}=128</annotation></semantics></math>) and (b) long prompt (<math id="S3.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}=2</annotation></semantics></math>k). The generation length is fixed at <math id="S3.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}=128</annotation></semantics></math>, and OOM denotes out-of-memory.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Real-world applications often serve multiple users concurrently, making scalability with respect to the batch size <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> a critical metric.
We therefore measure throughput (tokens/s) as a function of <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, fixing the generation length at <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}=128</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluating two prompt categories: short (<math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}=128</annotation></semantics></math>) and long (<math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}=2\text{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>k</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}=2\text{k}</annotation></semantics></math>).
We set the block size to <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=32</annotation></semantics></math> and use <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for the block-wise DLM.
All experiments in this section are run on a single NVIDIA A100 (80 GB) GPU to accommodate the memory demands of batched inference.
We omit the naive DLM, as prior work shows it does not benefit from batching due to its consistently <span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compute-bound</span> bidirectional full attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.5 Implications of Inference with Different Batch Sizes ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> indicates that for both ARMs and DLMs, throughput scaling with batch size diminishes as prompt length <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> increases.
As <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> grows, the memory-intensive task of reading a large <span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">KV cache</span> becomes the dominant part of the workload, limiting the potential throughput gains from batching.
Despite this shared trend, ARMs consistently exhibit better batching scalability than DLMs across all prompt lengths.
For example, at <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}=128</annotation></semantics></math>, ARM throughput scales effectively up to a batch size of 16, whereas DLM throughput already plateaus around 8 and remains much lower in absolute terms.
This disparity arises because the ARM’s lightweight, memory-bound decoding effectively leverages batch parallelism, while the DLM’s throughput is constrained by two factors: its compute-heavy block generation leads to an earlier performance plateau, and the high cumulative cost of iterative refinement results in a lower overall peak.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The plots also reveal memory limitations. For the block-wise DLM, out-of-memory (OOM) errors appear at smaller <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> grows.
Even though decoding proceeds block by block, attention within each block is bidirectional and requires the KV cache for the full sequence length <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=L_{p}+L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=L_{p}+L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to be resident.
Consequently, peak memory scales with <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\times L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\times L</annotation></semantics></math>, causing longer prompts to hit OOM at smaller batch sizes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Discussion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Autoregressive language models (ARMs) have driven major progress in NLP, but their next-token objective imposes sequential dependencies that increase latency and can degrade quality by limiting bidirectional context.
Diffusion language models (DLMs) are a promising alternative, as they have potential to decode many tokens in parallel, while conditioning on the full context.
We have presented a comprehensive analysis of the performance trade-offs between open-source ARMs and DLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis shows that a naive DLM exhibits substantial latency overheads relative to ARMs as the context length increases.
To ameliorate this, block-wise decoding with approximate KV caching offers an effective hybrid approach.
This method achieves increased arithmetic intensity (AI) by exploiting intra-block parallelism, while still scaling well to long contexts, similar to ARMs.
However, in batched serving, the block-wise DLM’s parallel generation saturates the GPU at smaller batch sizes than ARMs.
Combined with the DLM’s high cost of iterative refinement, this results in ARMs providing distinct throughput advantages in the large-batch regime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also observe that open-source DLM implementations typically lag behind open-source ARMs in speed.
In contrast, recent closed-source systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> report striking latency gains at comparable quality – often hundreds to thousands of times faster than current open-source DLMs, and even faster than ARMs.
Two core bottlenecks hinder open-source DLM performance: poor scaling with context length and high refinement costs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the scaling problem, Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Implications of Block-wise Decoding with KV Cache ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> confirms that block-wise decoding with approximate KV caching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> is an indispensable strategy.
Yet, to truly rival ARM speed, the most critical path forward involves reducing the number of sampling steps <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
In common configurations, <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> scales with the generation length <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to high per-output costs and compute saturation, as highlighted in Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 Implications of Inference with Different Batch Sizes ‣ 3 Workload Analysis ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.
Recent approaches attempt to cut steps without quality loss, such as multi-token finalization with autoregressive guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, confidence-based early finalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, and distillation into few-step or even one-step student models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
Reducing sampling steps is particularly promising in <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">small-batch</span> scenarios, where a DLM’s throughput could surpass an ARM’s, as mentioned in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Implications of Reduction in Sampling Steps ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
Finally, orthogonal system-level optimizations can offer additional speedups. These include compute-focused methods like low-precision execution as well as sparse attention methods that utilize only the most salient cache entries.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
