<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>RTX A6000 Roofline Parameters</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We derive the roofline parameters for the NVIDIA RTX A6000 (GA102).
We assume <em id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dense</em> FP16 Tensor Core computation (without 2:4 structured sparsity) and use the GPU boost clock.
Hardware specifications (SM count, Tensor Cores per SM, boost clock, memory bandwidth,
and FP16 per-Tensor Core throughput) are taken from the
GA102 architecture whitepaper.
<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          NVIDIA Ampere GA102 Architecture Whitepaper: <a href="https://www.nvidia.com/content/PDF/nvidia-ampere-ga-102-gpu-architecture-whitepaper-v2.pdf" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.nvidia.com/content/PDF/nvidia-ampere-ga-102-gpu-architecture-whitepaper-v2.pdf</a>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Peak FP16 Performance.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex2X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Peak FP16 (dense)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>Peak FP16 (dense)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Peak FP16 (dense)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex2X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=84~\mathrm{SM}\times 4~\mathrm{Tensor~Core/SM}\times 128~\mathrm{FMA/(cycle\cdot Tensor~Core)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>84</mn><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SM</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Tensor</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Core</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>SM</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>FMA</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cycle</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Tensor</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Core</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=84~\mathrm{SM}\times 4~\mathrm{Tensor~Core/SM}\times 128~\mathrm{FMA/(cycle\cdot Tensor~Core)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex2Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex2Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\times 1.8~\mathrm{GHz}\times 2~\mathrm{FLOP/FMA}\approx 154.8~\mathrm{TFLOP/s}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1.8</mn><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>GHz</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>FLOP</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>FMA</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>154.8</mn><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>TFLOP</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\times 1.8~\mathrm{GHz}\times 2~\mathrm{FLOP/FMA}\approx 154.8~\mathrm{TFLOP/s}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ridge Point.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With memory bandwidth <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="768~\mathrm{GB/s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>768</mn><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>GB</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">768~\mathrm{GB/s}</annotation></semantics></math>, the ridge arithmetic intensity is</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{AI}_{\mathrm{ridge}}=\frac{154.8~\mathrm{TFLOP/s}}{768~\mathrm{GB/s}}\approx 201.6~\mathrm{FLOP/Byte}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>AI</mi><mi>ridge</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>154.8</mn><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>TFLOP</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>768</mn><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>GB</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>201.6</mn><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>FLOP</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>Byte</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{AI}_{\mathrm{ridge}}=\frac{154.8~\mathrm{TFLOP/s}}{768~\mathrm{GB/s}}\approx 201.6~\mathrm{FLOP/Byte}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Implications of Reduction in Sampling Steps</h2>

<figure id="A2.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04146v1/batching_diffsteps.png" id="A2.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/250;" width="476" height="250" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>
<span id="A2.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Throughput vs. batch size for ARM and block-wise DLM with a varying number of sampling steps.</span>
The plots show the throughput of ARM and block-wise DLM for prompt length <math id="A2.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}\in\{128,512,2048\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>128,512</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}\in\{128,512,2048\}</annotation></semantics></math> with generation length <math id="A2.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}=128</annotation></semantics></math> and block size <math id="A2.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=32</annotation></semantics></math>. For LLaDA, <math id="A2.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of sampling steps, which is varied from the baseline of 128 down to 4.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As noted in our discussion, reducing the number of sampling steps <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a critical path toward making DLMs competitive. To quantify this potential, we conduct a batch scalability experiment where we mechanically reduce the number of sampling steps <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> from our default block-wise DLM configuration (block size <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=32</annotation></semantics></math>).
Specifically, we reduce <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> from its baseline of 128 (where <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>) down to 64, 32, 16, and 4. For example, when <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=4</annotation></semantics></math>, the generation length <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}=128</annotation></semantics></math> is divided into 4 blocks of 32 tokens each, and each block is denoised in a single refinement step.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>
Block-wise DLM performance on GSM8K and HumanEval benchmarks across different sampling steps <math id="A2.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (from 128 down to 4).
Generation length is fixed at <math id="A2.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}=128</annotation></semantics></math> and block size <math id="A2.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=32</annotation></semantics></math>.
GSM8K is reported as exact-match (EM) accuracy, and HumanEval is reported as pass@1.
</figcaption>
<table id="A2.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A2.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F5F5F5;">
<td id="A2.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F5F5F5;">Sampling Steps <math id="A2.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-bg-color:#F5F5F5;" mathbackground="#F5F5F5">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F5F5F5;">GSM8K (4-shot, EM)</span></td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F5F5F5;">HumanEval (0-shot, pass@1)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">128</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.755</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.323</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">64</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.719</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.232</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">32</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.612</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.067</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T2.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">16</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.360</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.049</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T2.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T2.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">4</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.069</td>
<td id="A2.T2.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.000</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to throughput analysis, we also evaluate accuracy degradation when reducing steps without any further techniques. We follow the evaluation setup of prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> and test on representative benchmarks: the GSM8K math benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and the HumanEval coding benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. As shown in Table <a href="#A2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Appendix B Implications of Reduction in Sampling Steps ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, naively reducing the number of steps results in severe accuracy drops, underscoring the necessity of advanced methods such as confidence-based multi-token finalization, autoregressive guidance, or distillation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix B Implications of Reduction in Sampling Steps ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the results, with memory footprint remaining unchanged since only the number of iterations is reduced. For instance, with a 128-token prompt, we observe that although performance still plateaus around a batch size of 8, the peak throughput scales nearly proportionally as <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> decreases.
These findings reinforce our earlier hypothesis in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Discussion and Future Work ‣ Beyond Next-Token Prediction: A Performance Characterization of Diffusion versus Autoregressive Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>: in the <span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">small-batch</span> regime where DLMs can still benefit from batching, reducing the number of sampling steps (especially via methods that finalize multiple tokens per step) has the potential to enable DLMs to surpass ARMs in throughput, provided quality degradation is mitigated by advanced techniques.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
